"B<             const void *_Nullable >I<value>B<, int >I<aux>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""
"B<int mount(const char *>I<source>B<, const char *>I<target>B<,>\n"
"B<          const char *>I<filesystemtype>B<, unsigned long >I<mountflags>B<,>\n"
"B<          const void *>I<data>B<);>\n"

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:24
msgid ""
"The B<fsconfig>()  system call is part of the suite of file-descriptor-based "
"mount facilities in Linux."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:35
msgid ""
"B<fsconfig>()  is used to supply parameters to and issue commands against "
"the filesystem configuration context associated with the file descriptor "
"I<fd>.  Filesystem configuration contexts can be created with B<fsopen>(2)  "
"or be instantiated from an extant filesystem instance with B<fspick>(2)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:46
msgid ""
"The I<cmd> argument indicates the command to be issued.  Some commands "
"supply parameters to the context (equivalent to mount options specified with "
"B<mount>(8)), while others are meta-operations on the filesystem context.  "
"The list of valid I<cmd> values are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:47
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<FSCONFIG_SET_FLAG>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:56
msgid ""
"Set the flag parameter named by I<key>.  I<value> must be NULL, and I<aux> "
"must be 0."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:56
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<FSCONFIG_SET_STRING>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:67
msgid ""
"Set the string parameter named by I<key> to the value specified by "
"I<value>.  I<value> points to a null-terminated string, and I<aux> must be 0."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:67
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<FSCONFIG_SET_BINARY>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:80
msgid ""
"Set the blob parameter named by I<key> to the contents of the binary blob "
"specified by I<value>.  I<value> points to the start of a buffer that is "
"I<aux> bytes in length."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:80
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "B<LOOP_SET_FD>"
msgid "B<FSCONFIG_SET_FD>"
msgstr "B<LOOP_SET_FD>"

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:89
msgid ""
"Set the file parameter named by I<key> to the open file description "
"referenced by the file descriptor I<aux>.  I<value> must be NULL."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:106
msgid ""
"You may also use B<\\%FSCONFIG_SET_STRING> for file parameters, with "
"I<value> set to a null-terminated string containing a base-10 representation "
"of the file descriptor number.  This mechanism is primarily intended for "
"compatibility with older B<mount>(2)-based programs, and only works for "
"parameters that I<only> accept file descriptor arguments."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:106
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "B<AT_EMPTY_PATH>"
msgid "B<FSCONFIG_SET_PATH>"
msgstr "B<AT_EMPTY_PATH>"

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:125
msgid ""
"Set the path parameter named by I<key> to the object at a provided path, "
"resolved in a similar manner to B<openat>(2).  I<value> points to a null-"
"terminated pathname string, and I<aux> is equivalent to the I<dirfd> "
"argument to B<openat>(2).  See B<openat>(2)  for an explanation of the need "
"for B<\\%FSCONFIG_SET_PATH>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:135
msgid ""
"You may also use B<\\%FSCONFIG_SET_STRING> for path parameters, the behavior "
"of which is equivalent to B<\\%FSCONFIG_SET_PATH> with I<aux> set to B<\\"
"%AT_FDCWD>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:135
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<FSCONFIG_SET_PATH_EMPTY>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:157
msgid ""
"As with B<\\%FSCONFIG_SET_PATH>, except that if I<value> is an empty string, "
"the file descriptor specified by I<aux> is operated on directly and may be "
"any type of file (not just a directory).  This is equivalent to the behavior "
"of B<\\%AT_EMPTY_PATH> with most \"*at()\" system calls.  If I<aux> is B<\\"
"%AT_FDCWD>, the parameter will be set to the current working directory of "
"the calling process."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:157
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<FSCONFIG_CMD_CREATE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:169
msgid ""
"This command instructs the filesystem driver to instantiate an instance of "
"the filesystem in the kernel with the parameters specified in the filesystem "
"configuration context.  I<key> and I<value> must be NULL, and I<aux> must be "
"0."
msgstr ""

#.  FS_CONTEXT_AWAITING_MOUNT is the term the kernel uses for this.
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:193
msgid ""
"This command can only be issued once in the lifetime of a filesystem "
"context.  If the operation succeeds, the filesystem context associated with "
"file descriptor I<fd> now references the created filesystem instance, and is "
"placed into a special \"awaiting-mount\" mode that allows you to use "
"B<fsmount>(2)  to create a mount object from the filesystem instance.  If "
"the operation fails, in most cases the filesystem context is placed in a "
"failed mode and cannot be used for any further B<fsconfig>()  operations "
"(though you may still retrieve diagnostic messages through the message "
"retrieval interface, as described in the corresponding subsection of "
"B<fsopen>(2))."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:202
msgid ""
"This command can only be issued against filesystem configuration contexts "
"that were created with B<fsopen>(2).  In order to create a filesystem "
"instance, the calling process must have the B<\\%CAP_SYS_ADMIN> capability."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:220
msgid ""
"An important thing to be aware of is that the Linux kernel will I<silently> "
"reuse extant filesystem instances depending on the filesystem type and the "
"configured parameters (each filesystem driver has its own policy for how "
"filesystem instances are reused).  This means that the filesystem instance "
"\"created\" by B<\\%FSCONFIG_CMD_CREATE> may, in fact, be a reference to an "
"extant filesystem instance in the kernel.  (For reference, this behavior "
"also applies to B<mount>(2).)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:243
msgid ""
"One side-effect of this behavior is that if an extant filesystem instance is "
"reused, I<all> parameters configured for this filesystem configuration "
"context are I<silently ignored> (with the exception of the I<ro> and I<rw> "
"flag parameters; if the state of the read-only flag in the extant filesystem "
"instance and the filesystem configuration context do not match, this "
"operation will return B<EBUSY>).  This also means that B<\\"
"%FSCONFIG_CMD_RECONFIGURE> commands issued against the \"created\" "
"filesystem instance will also affect any mount objects associated with the "
"extant filesystem instance."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:256
msgid ""
"Programs that need to ensure that they create a new filesystem instance with "
"specific parameters (notably, security-related parameters such as I<acl> to "
"enable POSIX ACLs\\[em]as described in B<acl>(5))  should use B<\\"
"%FSCONFIG_CMD_CREATE_EXCL> instead."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:256
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "B<LOOP_CONFIGURE> (since Linux 5.8)"
msgid "B<FSCONFIG_CMD_CREATE_EXCL> (since Linux 6.6)"
msgstr "B<LOOP_CONFIGURE> (Linux 5.8 以降)"

#.  commit 22ed7ecdaefe0cac0c6e6295e83048af60435b13
#.  commit 84ab1277ce5a90a8d1f377707d662ac43cc0918a
#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:270
msgid ""
"As with B<\\%FSCONFIG_CMD_CREATE>, except that the kernel is instructed to "
"not reuse extant filesystem instances.  If the operation would be forced to "
"reuse an extant filesystem instance, this operation will return B<EBUSY> "
"instead."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:279
msgid ""
"As a result (unlike B<\\%FSCONFIG_CMD_CREATE>), if this operation succeeds "
"then the calling process can be sure that all of the parameters successfully "
"configured with B<fsconfig>()  will actually be applied to the created "
"filesystem instance."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:279
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<FSCONFIG_CMD_RECONFIGURE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:292
msgid ""
"This command instructs the filesystem driver to apply the parameters "
"specified in the filesystem configuration context to the extant filesystem "
"instance referenced by the filesystem configuration context.  I<key> and "
"I<value> must be NULL, and I<aux> must be 0."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:306
msgid ""
"This is primarily intended for use with B<fspick>(2), but may also be used "
"to modify the parameters of a filesystem instance after B<\\"
"%FSCONFIG_CMD_CREATE> was used to create it and a mount object was created "
"using B<fsmount>(2).  In order to reconfigure an extant filesystem instance, "
"the calling process must have the B<CAP_SYS_ADMIN> capability."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:324
msgid ""
"If the operation succeeds, the filesystem context is reset but remains in "
"reconfiguration mode and thus can be reused for subsequent B<\\"
"%FSCONFIG_CMD_RECONFIGURE> commands.  If the operation fails, in most cases "
"the filesystem context is placed in a failed mode and cannot be used for any "
"further B<fsconfig>()  operations (though you may still retrieve diagnostic "
"messages through the message retrieval interface, as described in the "
"corresponding subsection of B<fsopen>(2))."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man2/fsconfig.2:334
msgid ""
"Parameters specified with B<FSCONFIG_SET_>I<*> do not take effect until a "
"corresponding B<\\%FSCONFIG_CMD_CREATE> or B<\\%FSCONFIG_CMD_RECONFIGURE> "
"command is issued."
msgstr ""

